{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the finding item at head 7b16690b8f, tree 0e4c2863, for request d711f37d and review request 53647034. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nThe head adds 12 paths to main a6b238fb. It's data only: a successor of `changeJ4` with one item type, four acts and one merge guard, and nothing under the kernel or platform changes, so no filing gate applies. `check-kernel-boundary` checks 127 sources with 0 refusals. I read the definition and the scenario's assertions, and I ran no independent second read.\n\n## Your five points\n\n1. Who answers: acceptable, including the later-version author. `change.dismiss` is held only by admin and maintainer (`membership.ts:107`), and those roles can already change the rules or dismiss a review. A filer resolving its own only withdraws its own input, and it could have filed nothing. A filer can't call its own finding irrelevant. The limit is real: someone with the grant who writes a later version can clear a finding on an earlier one. Keep it stated, as the note does.\n\n2. Sameness: acceptable as stated. A moved range can raise again, and the guard fails toward more findings, so it can hold a merge but never free one. `end` before `start` has no refusal, and the note says so.\n\n3. Not an approval: sound. `finding` adds no `review` item, and the definition test compares every other row whole with `changeJ4`, so no approval rule changed. C9's assertion is weaker, as you say, and the definition carries the claim.\n\n4. Merges in progress: acceptable, as stated. A late finding doesn't touch a merge already in flight.\n\n5. Rules change: acceptable. Current rules name the required sources by design, and rules changes need their own review. It does mean that whoever can publish rules can free a merge, and that's the same authority that sets approvals.\n\n## The request's assertions\n\nAll seven are in `finding.scope.test.ts`: an open required finding refuses merge, resolving allows it, irrelevant allows it, a non-required source doesn't refuse, another change refuses as `not-this-change`, a new version keeps it open, and an unauthorized member can't answer. A finding on a superseded version can still be answered there, since F1 was resolved after a new version. The second case publishes through the real destination.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The gate at c83c21cc exits 0. Its `test.log` shows Node 98, Counting 56, Plain 3,218 with one skip and Native 899 with two skips. From there to this head, only the note and the manifest change.\n- The controls ran at d2ddf228. From there to the checkpoint, the diff in the lane paths is the shared stand-in move and comment and reference wording, and the definition's rows don't change. All 14 retained control files say that they distinguish. I read those files and did not rerun them.\n- The evidence verifies. All 20 manifest rows match, the manifest has digest 1bd7b8a4, the directory has 58 files, its `SHA256SUMS` reruns clean with digest 83041f53, and no file is writable.\n- `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main a6b238fb gives tree 0e4c2863.\n- I ran `finding.scope.test.ts` (2 of 2) and `finding-definition.test.ts` (1 of 1) once each. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, `check-commit-messages` checks 6 with 0 flagged, `check-doc-links` on an export finds 775 links with 0 broken, and `git diff --check` is clean. The worktree stayed clean.\n\n## For the planner\n\nA room on this successor loses S6's Change card and S4's views for its change lanes, by the builder's reading of the code, which nobody has run and which I did not trace. Findings also show in no view. So this definition isn't usable in a real sprint room until those views accept the profile. File that view change before J1 needs it.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The cap of 256 findings is untested, and the note marks the sharing across sources as inferred.\n2. `file-finding` on a draft or a merged change has no control, as the note says.\n\n## Architecture\n\nA new pinned successor, with every other row compared whole. Old pins stay. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nNo authority widens. A finding is input and can only hold a merge. The two ways to free one are a source's own withdrawal and a grant that already sets review input aside, and the opener and the version's authors are excluded. Approved.\n\n## Simplification\n\nIt uses existing guard forms and adds no kernel code. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks hold. No Worker serves this definition, so no deployment follows.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ed1eae56532c9838f80ce64034a1d84861c058af","binding":"assigned","head":"7b16690b8fc9b9302a81feb182134d48a0d58733","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cac7d242f039b01b1981e2a87084c9750e9218fe\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:e18ad8a927a60acf684c7a85188688271501b078\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:cac7d242f039b01b1981e2a87084c9750e9218fe","review_path":"reviewguard@1","stale":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0e8d615c639fdd06f096889d18e2b0e4fa5e6c12, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1a43bfd3c196afdabbcd6e3a47843afbdeeecc79, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:221a17ae845080fff4a13cc7c8dffb39a1d576a4, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2a73363287d30105fae8cfba21436fbc8781e64e, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3e8edf533bd1b4d402c1658e60f1ed2e44340e18, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:536470348c4f73787feabd12ccde7a51041c6958, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:55e7227863909e6824311747509eeeb8ca8ff5bb, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git